s generally how the  training setup looks like we typically  assume a set of training mdps  and then we want to be able to  generalize to a new mdp from that same  distribution  a common approach that we'll focus on  today for solving this problem  is to implement the learning procedure  using a recurrent neural network using  kind of a black box  neural network and what this looks like  it might be something like this where  you have this neural network it  takes as input states and rewards that  it receives over time  and produces the next action and the  exploration strategy  and the exploitation strategy is built  into  this model  you might ask well okay is this just a  recurrent policy is this like not really  anything  different from that uh and kind of the  key difference is that the hidden state  is going to be maintained across  episodes within an mdp so they can  remember  the experience that it has learned and  also remember the rewards that is  received as passing as input  and second this recurrent network policy  will be traded across a family  of mdps potentially with varying  dynamics and varying rewards  and it has to infer for a given mdp what  is  what is the strategy for solving that  mdp potentially implicitly learning  something about the reward function and  something about the dynamics  okay so that was a quick kind of primer  on better reinforcement learning  now a sub problem with meta  reinforcement learning is trying to  figure out  how this policy or how this meta policy  should explore the environment when  faced with a new mdp  so the really kind of vanilla approach  to solving this problem  is just to optimize for exploration and  exploitation  end to end with respect to reward  or potentially more specifically with  respect to your kind of overall  regret  and this is really kind of the  widespread approach that's used by most  reinforced meta reinforcement learning  algorithms  and you're essentially just optimizing  that big recurrent neural network such  that  it leads to um to kind of  large cumulative reward and this is nice  in that it's very simple we just have to  train it with regards to this kind of  final objective it also in principle  will lead to the optimal  exploration and exploitation strategy uh  assuming that your current network has  enough capacity and you're off you're  able to actually optimize that  but in practice and also actually in  theory  there's actually a really challenging  optimization when you're faced with a  hard  exploration problem now why is this the  case why is it really hard to learn how  to explore  um to explain this let's look at an  example so  one example a reinforcement learning  problem might look like as  follows say we want a robot to be able  to learn cooking tasks  uh in a new kitchen and so what we do is  we train it across a lot of different  kinds of kitchens  learn many different kinds of tasks  and then after learning in all these  kitchens we want to deploy  the robot in our own kitchen and have it  be able to quickly learn to  cook different things for us in our own  kitchen so this is an example  essentially of a mother reinforcement  learning problem  where we learned how to learn in all of  the training kitchens and then  tried to transfer to the new kitchen net  test time  so this is a pretty challenging  exploration problem because when  if you're in a new kitchen you need to  figure out where all the ingredients are  where all the tools  are and so forth and so you may need to  explore lots of different things  now why might it be hard to learn  exploration strategies that are  effective in this type of situation  so the end-to-end approach is going to  be optimizing for exploration and  execution  end-to-end to maximize reward and  you end up getting this chicken and egg  problem where the execution or the  exploitation it needs to learn how to  cook  and the exploration needs to learn how  to find ingredients  and both of these rely on each other  so if you don't know how to find  ingredients if you have a bad  exploration strategy  then it's going to be hard to learn how  to cook because you don't have enough  information  to solve the task and likewise if you  haven't yet learned how to cook  then that's going to give you very low  reward for any exploration strategy  that you may consider so this is a  we have this sort of coupling problem  which is actually pretty big problem in  practice too  which is that learning the exploration  and the execution depend on one another  and this leads to both potentially local  optimum  in the optimization landscape  and or very poor sample complexity when  you actually try to learn these  end to end  okay so how might we try to address this  challenge  first i'll overview some recent  approaches for  for addressing this and then i'll talk  about an approach that i think is a bit  more satisfying  so one solution to addressing this  chicken and egg problem  is to leverage alternative exploration  strategies  when exploring each new mvp  rather than trying to optimize  exploration end to end  so one example of this might look like  trying to use posterior sampling or  thompson sampling  when faced with a new task so what this  looks like  is these approaches learn a  distribution over a latent variable that  captures the variation in your mpps or  your tasks  and they also learn a corresponding  policy for each of those latent  variables that will solve each of those  tasks  and then once you learn this kind of  latent task variable and the  corresponding task policies  then you can sample from this  distribution from your kind of current  posterior  over your past distribution and then  sample from the  corresponding policies uh so this  basically exactly corresponds to  to thompson sampling except that we're  doing it in this latent space  over the kind of distribution over tasks  that we  over the tasks in our meta training set  or over the tas