licy that's much  more efficient that takes a strategy  that looks like this  but of course there are also situations  where this also this won't work well  for example if you have lots of  distractors or a very complex  high-dimensional state space  then learning this model may be very  challenging  and your exploration policy may try to  focus on distractors that vary across  the tasks  and not actually task relevant  information  so essentially these are kind of a  collection of more heuristic approaches  that aren't fairly principled but may be  inefficient when learning to explore so  the upside is that these approaches are  very easy to optimize because they  in some ways decouple the exploration  problem in the inner loop  from the outer meta optimization  and many of them are based on principled  strategies but they can be  sub-optimal by arbitrarily large amounts  in some environment  okay so with this kind of overview of  how metal earning algorithms learn how  to explore  what we want to try to solve is trying  to figure out if we could avoid this  chicken and egg problem  without sacrificing optimality  of our learned exploration strategies  and the answer to this question is yes  we can actually do this and i'll  next talk about some work that was done  by my student evan  that was trying to solve this problem  and the key idea of this approach is  that we're going to try to decouple  the the exploration and the exploitation  by acquiring a representation of task  relevant information  so the way that this works is  in the meta trading process we have a  set of mdps  that we want to learn how to explore and  solve  and first we learn a policy that can  execute each of the tasks and  simultaneously  identify key information about those  tasks  so the way this works is we have some  identifier of the mdp  that may be a one-hot vector or maybe  slightly more descriptive  and we learn an execution policy that  can solve each of our training mdps  conditione